※※ What every program starts with, and the devices it starts with open.
※
※ One module for now, named `std` and found beside the compiler.  What a program
※ names here is a *descriptor* -- something that may be read, or written, or
※ both -- and never the machinery underneath it.
※
※ The machinery is the runtime the compiler carries: C, compiled ahead of time
※ for every architecture, reached through `@[external]` and handed the ring as a
※ record laid out the way it reads one.  That there is a ring, and that there is
※ sometimes not one, is not something a program can see.

※※ A device that can be read.
@[export]
type Reader = fd : i32

※※ A device that can be written.
@[export]
type Writer = fd : i32

※※ A device that can be both, which is what a socket and a file opened either
※※ way are.
@[export]
type ReadWriter = fd : i32

※※ The devices a program starts with open.
@[export]
type Io = input : Reader ; output : Writer ; errors : Writer

※※ What a program is started with.  A record rather than the descriptors
※※ themselves, so that the arguments, the environment and whatever else a
※※ program inherits have somewhere to go without changing the one signature
※※ every program writes.
@[export]
type Init = io : Io

※ -- the ring ---------------------------------------------------------------

※※ What the runtime keeps about the ring, laid out the way it reads it.
※
※ Every field is a machine word, so that this declaration and the one in
※ `runtime/io.c` are the same by inspection rather than by argument.  Nothing
※ here reads any of it: what this module does with it is hand over a reference,
※ which is what an `@[abi]` record does and all it does.
@[abi]
type Ring =
    state : u64 ; fd : u64 ;
    sq_head : u64 ; sq_tail : u64 ; sq_mask : u64 ; sq_array : u64 ;
    sqes : u64 ;
    cq_head : u64 ; cq_tail : u64 ; cq_mask : u64 ; cqes : u64

※※ The one ring, made by the runtime the first time anything reads or writes.
let ring: mut Ring = Ring(
    .state ← 0u64, .fd ← 0u64,
    .sq_head ← 0u64, .sq_tail ← 0u64, .sq_mask ← 0u64, .sq_array ← 0u64,
    .sqes ← 0u64,
    .cq_head ← 0u64, .cq_tail ← 0u64, .cq_mask ← 0u64, .cqes ← 0u64)

@[external("pl4g_io_write"), impure]
fn io_write(r: &mut Ring, fd: i32, at: u64, len: u64) → i64

@[external("pl4g_io_read"), impure]
fn io_read(r: &mut Ring, fd: i32, at: u64, len: u64) → i64

※ -- what a program calls ---------------------------------------------------

※※ Write `what` to `to`, answering how many bytes went or what the kernel said.
※
※ The reference is exclusive, which is what says nothing else is writing to the
※ same device at the same time; it is checked where it is taken and costs
※ nothing here.
@[export, impure]
fn write(to: &mut Writer, what: u8⟦⟧) → i64:
    ※ Nothing to write is nothing done, and asking for the address of the first
    ※ of no elements is asking for a place that is not there.
    if #what = 0:
        return 0i64
    io_write(&mut ring, to⌖.fd, ⎕address(&what⟦0⟧), ⎕drop(#what))

※※ Read from `from` into `into`, answering how many bytes came or what the
※※ kernel said.  Nought means there is no more to come.
@[export, impure]
fn read(from: &mut Reader, into: u8⟦⟧) → i64:
    ※ Nowhere to put anything is nothing read, for the reason above.
    if #into = 0:
        return 0i64
    io_read(&mut ring, from⌖.fd, ⎕address(&into⟦0⟧), ⎕drop(#into))
